  "turns": [
    {
      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello. I am Dr. Smith. What brings you in to see me today?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S019",
      "patient_response": "Hello, Dr. Smith. Well, I suppose... gosh, where do I even start? I mean, I've been struggling a bit in general, just feeling rather low, but I came in today because... well, even on my better days, the days where I can actually get up and get myself put together and out of the house, I just can't shake this awful feeling. \n\nIt's about the people at my church—I've been going to the same congregation for over thirty years, mind you, ever since my husband and I bought our house—and also the ladies in my Tuesday morning walking club. We usually walk around the indoor mall before the shops open, just to keep our joints moving. But lately, I can just tell they are watching me. And it's not just the staring, it's... well, they put these little messages about me in things. \n\nLike at church, in the Sunday bulletin, I'll be sitting in the pew reading the weekly announcements or the prayer list, and I can see these hidden remarks that are clearly, specifically directed at me. And then at the walking club, it's the exact same thing. They'll be talking, just chatting about the weather or their gardens, but I can hear it in what they say. They slip these little messages about me right into their sentences, disguised as normal conversation. Even when my mood is a little lighter and I'm trying to just enjoy the walk or the church service, I just can't ignore it. It makes me so uncomfortable, always having to read between the lines, and I don't understand why they've all decided to do this to me. So... I guess that's why I'm here. It's just exhausting.",
