    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Aside from the hidden meanings you perceive in their words, do you ever hear voices when no one else is around?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S020",
      "patient_response": "Well, yes... yes, I suppose I do. It’s the strangest thing, Dr. Smith. You see, when the house gets completely quiet—you know, no television on, no radio playing, just that heavy sort of silence you get in the mid-afternoon? I'll just be sitting in my armchair in the living room, maybe doing a bit of cross-stitch or just resting my eyes because my energy has been so terribly low lately, and... well, I will hear my name. \n\nJust out of the blue. Or sometimes it's a few words, like a partial sentence. And the thing that really gets me is that it's not just some random sound, it's actually in my friend's voice. Usually one of the ladies from the walking club. I'll hear it coming right from the kitchen, or sometimes out on the back porch. \n\nAnd it is so clear, so perfectly normal and clear, that I don't even think twice about it. I actually answer back! I'll call out, \"I'm in the living room!\" or \"Did you need something?\" and I'll put my sewing down and get all the way up to go look. And it's only when I get to the kitchen doorway that I stop and remember... well, nobody is there. The doors are locked. But I swear to you, in that moment, it sounds exactly like they are standing right there by my refrigerator talking to me. It gives me such a start, my heart just pounds, because I really thought they had come over, and then I'm just standing there alone in the quiet again.",
