    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "When you look back over the last few months, have these feelings of low energy and sadness been present every single day, even on the days when you aren't feeling particularly worried about the messages from your friends?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_2",
      "patient_response": "Well, yes, Doctor... yes, I suppose they have. It’s hard to untangle it all sometimes, you know? But when I really sit down and think about it, look back over the last... oh, gosh, it must be months now. Since before Thanksgiving, certainly. My sister called around then, and I just couldn't even bring myself to chat about the turkey or the stuffing, which is so unlike me because I usually host, you see. \n\nBut yes, to answer your question, even on the days when the ladies at the walking club aren't putting those awful hidden meanings into their sentences about their hydrangeas, or when I don't hear my name being called from the empty kitchen... even then, this heavy, awful feeling is just sitting right there on my chest. It's like a damp blanket that I just can't throw off. The sadness is always there, just humming away in the background. And the guilt! Oh, the guilt is terrible. I'll sit in my chair and just feel so terribly guilty about not keeping the house up the way my late husband liked it, or not calling my sister back. \n\nAnd I just don't care about the things I used to. The cross-stitch I mentioned? I used to make the most beautiful ornaments for the church bazaar. Every year, people would ask for them. But now, my basket just sits there. I look at the thread and I just... I don't care. Even when my mind isn't racing with what the Sunday bulletin really meant, or who might be standing on the back porch, I still just can't find the joy in the thread. So, yes, for most of this whole long stretch, that terrible sadness and the guilt and just not caring about my little hobbies... it's all been right there in the background, plain as day, even when the other things are quiet. It's just so draining, Dr. Smith. I used to have so much energy, you know. I'd be up at dawn, baking...",
