      "doctor_question": "Have there been any periods of at least two weeks where you felt your mood was relatively normal, but you were still experiencing these thoughts about the bulletin or hearing those voices?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_3",
      "patient_response": "Well, yes, actually... now that you mention it, there was a time. It was, oh, maybe right around the beginning of February? The weather was just starting to turn a little less bitter, and I remember thinking that maybe, just maybe, the worst of the winter slump was behind me. For a few weeks there—maybe three or four weeks, if I'm remembering right—the heaviness actually lifted a bit. I wasn't feeling quite so weighed down by that terrible sadness, and the guilt wasn't pressing on me quite so hard. \n\nI was actually getting up and getting dressed properly, putting on a nice blouse instead of just my housecoat, and I was even eating a bit better. I made a proper pot roast one Sunday, with the carrots and the potatoes just the way I like them, which I hadn't done in months. I thought, \"Oh, finally, I'm getting back to my old self.\" \n\nBut the strange thing, Dr. Smith, the really confusing part of it all, is that even during those few weeks when my mood was so much better and I had a bit more spring in my step... the other things didn't go away at all. I was still sitting in my armchair in the afternoon, feeling perfectly content, and I would still hear my friend's voice calling my name from the kitchen. Clear as a bell. And I'd still get up and look, and no one would be there. \n\nAnd I still went to church those weeks, feeling quite proud of myself for getting there early, but the moment I opened the Sunday bulletin, there they were—those same little hidden remarks, those hints about me tucked right into the announcements about the choir practice and the bake sale. It was so frustrating! Because I felt so much better in myself, you know, my spirit felt lighter, but I still had to deal with the ladies at the walking club slipping those messages into their chatter about their grandchildren. So yes, there was definitely a spell of several weeks where the sadness took a back seat, but the voices and the hints... they stayed right there with me the whole time.",
